"A Proof of Rubey's Lattice Conjecture - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2507.18852v3)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; ## A Proof of Rubey's Lattice Conjecture ... In 2011, Rubey generalized chute and ladder moves on the set of reduced pipe dreams for a permutation $w$ and conjectured that the induced poset on reduced pipe dreams is a lattice.\n\n## A Proof of Rubey's Lattice Conjecture\n\nSara C. Billey, Connor McCausland, Clare Minnerath\n\nPublished: 2026-10-01 | Updated: 2026-10-02\n\nComments: 39 pages, 17 figures. To be published in Combinatorial Theory. Several figures have been revised, and the proofs of Lemma 4.5 and Theorem 1.2 have been simplified. Further discussion of the Markov process on the Rubey lattice has been given in Section 6\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn 2011, Rubey generalized chute and ladder moves on the set of reduced pipe dreams for a permutation $w$ and conjectured that the induced poset on reduced pipe dreams is a lattice. In this paper, we prove this conjecture. Our key tool is a new type of move operation $\\mathcal{M}_{ij}$, defined as a composite of certain generalized ladder moves and then simplified in terms of swaps on a partition shape. We show that joins and meets exist in Rubey's poset by proving simple recursive formulas in terms of $\\mathcal{M}_{ij}$ operations, so we refer to the poset as the Rubey lattice henceforth. In addition, we give an explicit criterion to determine if two elements of the Rubey lattice are comparable using an injective map from reduced pipe dreams to tableaux on the diagram of a permutation. The pipe dream tableaux construction also gives an exact formula for the maximum length of any chain in the Rubey lattice and bounds on the number of reduced pipe dreams for $w$, or equivalently the sum of the coefficients in the corresponding Schubert polynomial. Several open problems for further development of the Rubey lattice related to Markov processes, Schubert polynomials, and Grothendieck polynomials are given.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeakly pancyclic graphs - Brandt - 1998 - Journal of Graph Theory - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1002/%28SICI%291097-0118%28199803%2927%3A3%3C141%3A%3AAID-JGT3%3E3.0.CO%3B2-O)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Stephan Brandt, ... From the main result of this paper it follows that 2-connected nonbipartite graphs of sufficiently large order n with minimum degree exceeding 2n/7 are weakly pancyclic; and that graphs with minimum degree at least n/4 + 250 are pancyclic, if they contain both a triangle and a hamiltonian cycle.\n\nVolume 27, Issue 3 pp. 141-176\n\n# Weakly pancyclic graphs\n\nStephan Brandt, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nStephan Brandt\n\n  * brandt@math.fu-berlin.de\n\nFB Mathematik, Freie Universität Berlin, Graduiertenkolleg ‘Algorithmische Diskrete Mathematik’, Arnimallee 2-6, 14195 Berlin, Germany\n\n\nFirst published: 07 December 1998\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/(SICI)1097-0118(199803)27:3<141::AID-JGT3>3.0.CO;2-ODigital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn generalizing the concept of a pancyclic graph, we say that a graph is “weakly pancyclic” if it contains cycles of every length between the length of a shortest and a longest cycle. In this paper it is shown that in many cases the requirements on a graph which ensure that it is weakly pancyclic are considerably weaker than those required to ensure that it is pancyclic. This sheds some light on the content of a famous metaconjecture of Bondy. From the main result of this paper it follows that 2-connected nonbipartite graphs of sufficiently large order n with minimum degree exceeding 2n/7 are weakly pancyclic; and that graphs with minimum degree at least n/4 + 250 are pancyclic, if they contain both a triangle and a hamiltonian cycle. © 1998 